+
+The week should be remembered less as “many failed fixes” than as a
+sequence that finally changed the object of study. Before Jul 26, the
+program was optimizing a model. After Jul 26, the live question became:
+
+> What discrete surface measure is the model actually operating on,
+> and does every layer approximate one resolution-independent
+> continuum map?
+
+That is the right question. It should now govern the queue.
+
+### The eight findings that change the program
+
+#### 1. The effective-measure intervention does not reach the model
+
+This is the highest-severity correctness finding.
+
+`MeshTransformer.encode` constructs geometry-only boundary meshes with
+`with_data(point_data={}, cell_data={}, global_data={})`. That deletes
+`MEASURE_WEIGHTS_KEY`, the public cell-data entry carrying composed
+Horvitz–Thompson or other effective weights. It then creates a fresh
+`source_mesh` from normalized points and cells. Consequently:
+
+1. upstream effective weights are deleted before attention or decoding;
+2. `source_mesh.cell_areas` is bare geometric area;
+3. `measure_normalization` normalizes those reconstructed geometric
+   areas, not the represented effective measure;
+4. `nondim_measure` is never used after the normalization block, despite
+   the comment that magnitude “re-enters as the dimensionless global
+   scalar below.”
+
+An audit-only public-API probe attached both a uniform 880× factor,
+replicating the historical campaign convention, and nonuniform
+0.2-to-5.0 factors with `compose_measure_weights`. Outputs were bitwise
+identical with normalization on and off, and with zero or eight learned
+kernel members. That is not measure invariance; it is measure blindness.
+
+The 880× value carries a separate bookkeeping mismatch. The historical
+diagnostic calls the denominator 8.8 million **cells**, and preprocessing
+documentation describes roughly 8.8 million quad-dominant cells before
+triangulation. The curated reader later reports roughly 17.7 million
+triangles for the cited vehicle case. The sampler draws the latter cell
+population, so its uniform inclusion factor is the case-specific
+$N_\mathrm{cells}/n_\mathrm{sample}$, roughly 1,770 there. This
+pre-/post-triangulation counting mismatch makes the requested data
+contract more important, not less.
+
+There is also a direct counterexample to the later categorical claim
+that weights cannot help. On a 5,120-panel sphere with constant-density
+exact double-layer truth $-1$, an audit probe drew 512 uniform
+320-panel samples:
+
+| estimator | sample mean | observed mean error | design bias |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| bare retained-panel sum | $-0.06225$ | 93.8% | 93.75% |
+| HT-weighted sum | $-0.99595$ | 0.4% | 0% |
+
+Equal first-order inclusion makes the HT estimator design-unbiased for
+this full discrete panel total; 0.4% is finite Monte Carlo error of the
+reported sample mean, not estimator bias. Its standard error was 0.0105
+**for the mean over 512 replicate samples**, so a normal 95% interval
+is approximately $[-1.0165,-0.9754]$ and contains $-1$. It implies
+a single-draw standard deviation, and hence a roughly 0.238
+standard-deviation contribution to RMSE, but the audit did not retain
+the individual draws needed for empirical RMSE or quantiles. Those must
+become part of the durable artifact before making an estimator-quality
+claim. This does not show that HT weighting solves a trained nonlinear
+model; finite-sample variance, nonlinear plug-in bias, and
+representation shift remain. It does show that the exact linear
+component is reweightable and that “weights cannot fix it” is false as a
+universal statement.
+
+The tests missed this because they monkeypatch `Mesh.cell_areas`,
+changing physical panel area globally instead of attaching
+`MEASURE_WEIGHTS_KEY` to an input mesh. The flagship path is weaker
+still:
+
+- exact invariance is asserted only for `kernel_mlp_members=0`;
+- the production flagship uses eight learned members;
+- its “partial invariance” test permits relative drift below 50;
+- the all-boundary test again hardcodes zero learned members;
+- `test_off_is_bitwise_default` does not compare two outputs;
+- the H-BOTH composition test does not rescale a measure.
+
+Therefore the following claims are invalid as written:
+
+- “measure units are now fixed”;
+- “the magnitude re-enters as a global scalar”;
+- “the HT distribution survives”;
+- “H-BOTH has both required invariances”;
+- “HT weights are inert, therefore no weights can help.”
+
+The last statement may eventually be true for a particular estimator,
+but the current implementation never ran that experiment. The correct
+status is: **the production model ignores incoming effective weights;
+the proposed finite-sample correction remains untested.**
+
+#### 2. The intrinsic-gauge calibration leaked the held-out split
+
+`calibrate_intrinsic_gauge.py` sets `infer_split=train`, but iterates
+`range(len(val_loader.dataset))`. In manifest mode, the dataset contains
+all 484 cases; the sampler carries the 435/49 split. The normal
+inference loop correctly iterates `val_loader.sampler`.
+
+The saved artifact reports `n_samples: 484` and labels it `"split":
+"train"`. The book simultaneously says the standard training split has
+435 cars and that the constant was fit once on train and “must never”
+see evaluation data. Thus
+
+$$
+C = 26.476592786355283
+$$
+
+was fit on all 435 training plus 49 held-out cases.
+
+This is a real protocol breach, but it does **not** rehabilitate the
+gauge hypothesis. Transfer-v2 missed its structural bar by three to
+thirteen orders of magnitude, and later pattern diagnostics show
+near-orthogonal fields. A small change in $C$ cannot plausibly reverse
+that. The required response is to fix, recompute, quantify the delta,
+and correct the record—not to rerun the entire failed transfer program
+before measuring that delta.
+
+There is a second statistical overclaim in the gauge implementation
+documentation. HT weighting is unbiased for discrete totals, not for a
+centered ratio followed by a square root. With a uniform factor on one
+boundary it cancels exactly and leaves the sampled RMS radius. Call this
+statistic consistent or empirically subsample-robust only after measuring
+its cyclic-block bias and variance; do not call it an unbiased estimate
+of the full-resolution radius.
+
+#### 3. Chained jobs do not follow the same training trajectory
+
+The notebook calls two- and four-hour checkpoint chains
+“protocol-neutral—same trajectory.” The trainer saves the optimizer and
+scheduler before the epoch-level `scheduler.step()`, then resumes at the
+next epoch. The checkpoint therefore restores the pre-step scheduler
+state while skipping the epoch whose step was omitted. Every restart
+drops one schedule update.
+
+The default flagship uses `StepLR(step_size=100, gamma=0.1)`. Runs that
+hit different numbers or locations of wall-time boundaries can cross LR
+decays at different effective epochs. This matters because the July 25
+arms explicitly resumed after repeated SIGTERM boundaries and because
+seed and architecture runtimes need not be identical.
+
+There is a second reporting mismatch: training completes epoch 499/500,
+but no final checkpoint is saved. With the hidden campaign
+`save_interval=10`, inference loads `checkpoint.0.491.pt`. The prose
+mixes epoch-499 training validation with epoch-491 inference as “final”
+results, and the exact override is not checked in.
+
+Until a continuous-versus-resumed equivalence test exists, “matched
+protocol” must include the effective LR sequence and restart history,
+not just the nominal YAML.
+
+#### 4. The industrial evidence is not independently reconstructable
+
+The only last-week JSON under `mesh_transformer/results/` is
+`crossfamily_transfer_2026-07-22.json`. It says explicitly that it is a
+manual transcription from the notebook. It contains no resolved config,
+command, environment, tree digest, dirty diff, checkpoint hash, manifest
+hash, case IDs, or per-case values, and it stops before the transfer-v2
+verdict.
+
+The later transfer-v2, resolution, wave, five-seed, and pattern results
+live only in prose or remote scratch output. Headline treatment configs
+for `measure_normalization`, `field_mode: homogeneous`, `quadratic`, and
+H-BOTH are not tracked. The notebook records that they were trained from
+separate byte-copy trees with manual patches; those trees have no `.git`,
+saved diff, or source digest. The current flagship config remains
+nonlinear with eight learned members.
+
+This creates three distinct uncertainties:
+
+- Was current HEAD the code that generated a number?
+- Did treatment and reused controls run the same code and schedule?
+- Can a case-level metric be recomputed under a corrected definition?
+
+For the post-Jul-24 industrial wave, the answer is generally no. Those
+numbers can guide exploration, but they cannot support a paper or a
+“sealed” claim.
+
+#### 5. Barnes–Hut violates its stated similarity contract
+
+The Barnes–Hut module says it is similarity-equivariant because its tree
+lives in the normalized frame. Its Morton/AABB tree, node membership,
+axis-aligned diameter, and AABB-gap opening rule are coordinate-axis
+dependent. Normalizing length does not make an axis-aligned hierarchy
+rotation-equivariant.
+
+An audit-only end-to-end rotation probe found dense residuals at roundoff, while
+Barnes–Hut produced approximately:
+
+| $\theta$ | scalar rotation residual | vector rotation residual |
+|---:|---:|---:|
+| 0.25 | 1.2% | 4.1% |
+| 0.50 | 3.1% | 10.5% |
+| 1.00 | 6.8% | 17.2% |
+
+No rotation test exists in the Barnes–Hut acceptance suite. This does
+not damage the default dense backend, and Barnes–Hut already has no
+measured speed crossover at current product sizes. It does mean the
+backend must be parked or its contract weakened until the approximation
+error explicitly includes rotation dependence.
+
+It also constrains the proposed hierarchy pivot below: do not turn the
+current axis-aligned tree directly into the scientific coarsener and
+silently sacrifice a core contract.
+
+#### 6. “Exact-members-only” is not a pure layer-potential model
+
+The book's strongest mechanism language says the two exact singular
+members carry propagation while operator-conditioned source
+coefficients carry the unknown PDE. Removing learned kernel members
+does not isolate that graph.
+
+Even in exact-only mode:
+
+- queries pass through a nonlinear typed geometry network;
+- global drives have a direct query-side path that bypasses the kernel;
+- the summed kernel field passes through a learned
+  query-geometry-conditioned projection;
+- that projection contains invariant gates, field–geometry
+  contractions, and scalar–vector products.
+
+A learned query gate can multiply or contract a harmonic layer
+potential into a non-harmonic field. The project's own far-field work
+found this path load-bearing. Thus dictionary factorials measure the
+**marginal value of members conditional on an active query network**.
+They do not show that a two-member layer potential carries the full
+prediction.
+
+The theoretical license is also narrower than the book sometimes
+states. Classical Green/layer-potential representations and
+second-kind Fredholm density solves apply under PDE-specific
+conditions, most cleanly to linear elliptic problems with the
+appropriate Green function. They are not a PDE-agnostic theorem for
+Liouville, Euler, RANS, or a general unknown nonlinear operator. The
+hardcoded exact members are Laplace single and double layers, not the
+Green functions of all benchmark families.
+
+The surviving claim is still useful: the model is a typed,
+symmetry-aware boundary surrogate with a Laplace-like singular prior.
+To establish a stronger BIE mechanism, run a factorial between:
+
+1. a true linear layer-potential output with no query gate or direct
+   query drive;
+2. output gate only;
+3. direct query-drive path only;
+4. the full current graph.
+
+Report both field error and PDE residual. This experiment is more
+informative than another dictionary-member sweep.
+
+#### 7. The homogeneity contract has both a sign and a floor caveat
+
+The homogeneous read-in computes its unit state with
+$1/(\lVert x\rVert+\varepsilon)$, where the default
+$\varepsilon=10^{-30}$. It is therefore only approximately
+positive-homogeneous when $\lVert x\rVert\gg\varepsilon$, not exactly
+homogeneous for every positive scale. An audit probe at
+$\lVert x\rVert=10^{-30}$ found a relative scaling residual of 0.5;
+the existing tests use ordinary magnitudes and miss the floor.
+
+Nor is oddness guaranteed after training. The last MLP layer is
+zero-initialized, so a fresh module begins as the identity, but a
+learned nonlinear map of the normalized direction need not satisfy
+$f(-x)=-f(x)$. Tests use only $k>0$, while public prose writes
+$u(\lambda g)=\lambda u(g)$ without restricting either $\lambda$
+or the magnitude range.
+
+Either implement an exact nonzero branch and state $\lambda>0$, or
+document an approximate contract with a tested lower-magnitude domain.
+Add oddness only for physics that license sign reversal. None of these
+changes rescues homogeneity as a geometry-transfer remedy.
+
+#### 8. The production sampler is not the random sampler in the probe
+
+`_subsample_mesh_cells` does not draw a simple random subset or
+`randperm`. It selects one cyclic contiguous block of $k$ entries in
+the on-disk cell tensor from a uniformly random start. Every cell has
+marginal inclusion probability $k/N$, so the stored HT factor is
+valid for totals under repeated starts, but joint inclusion,
+spatial coverage, and variance depend on cell ordering.
+
+The core reader recommends pre-shuffling **point** order for its separate
+point-subsampling mode; it makes no equivalent guarantee for cell order.
+Recipe-local comments assert that the curated DrivAerML cell order is
+shuffled, but the audit found no durable measurement of its spatial
+representativeness.
+The sphere diagnostics instead use simple-random subsets. Therefore
+their bias/variance curves do not reproduce the production design.
+
+The frequently quoted 0.06% is also a **cell-count fraction**, not a
+measured surface-area or fill-distance coverage. Before attributing the
+resolution cliff to random decimation, measure for every relevant case
+and start:
+
+- retained geometric-area fraction and its spatial distribution;
+- fill distance / nearest-retained-cell distance on the full surface;
+- autocorrelation of area, normals, and labels in on-disk cell order;
+- cyclic-block versus simple-random versus conforming-cover behavior.
+
+The live hypothesis remains that sparse support is harmful. The
+production mechanism is now more specific and less settled:
+**cyclic-block subsampling of an unverified cell order**, not generic
+random decimation.
+
+### Experimental judgment: what was done well, and what must change
+
+#### What was done well
+
+The record's strongest feature is adversarial honesty. It preserves
+failed predictions, corrects exaggerated effects, reports excluded
+seeds, and repeatedly seeks disconfirmation. Specific examples include:
+
+- the five-seed homogeneous result was not rescued after missing the
+  declared 3× band by 2,491×;
+- the gauge dose-response was labeled exploratory before its later
+  refutation;
+- the 41× singularity-subtraction claim was independently replicated
+  and corrected downward by 4–10×;
+- the Q2 statistic was retired after it was shown to conflate collapse
+  and divergence;
+- H-SEED was attacked with an older experiment before commissioning a
+  new one;
+- the final notebook states its random-init and single-geometry caveats.
+
+This is why the program is recoverable. Negative results have not been
+buried.
+
+#### Where judgment failed
+
+The recurring error was **premature causal compression**: a dramatic
+observation was quickly assigned one mechanism and then encoded as a
+preregistered architecture wave before the relevant variables and
+metrics were isolated.
+
+##### The same SHIFT pilot became a development set
+
+The first 20-case SHIFT-SUV test was a legitimate untouched
+cross-family falsifier. The same cases were then used for:
+
+- the gauge scan;
+- transfer-v2;
+- four architecture arms;
+- the homogeneous seed extension;
+- metric validation;
+- amplitude and pattern diagnosis.
+
+Fresh predictions do not make the labels or family untouched again.
+After the gauge scan, SHIFT-SUV was a development set. Transfer-v2's
+failure is so enormous that its qualitative negative result is
+credible, but later “confirmatory” language is not.
+
+##### Drive degree was asked to solve the wrong invariance
+
+For positive $\lambda$ and drive magnitudes well above the
+implementation floor, `field_mode="homogeneous"` approximately enforces
+
+$$
+u(\lambda g)=\lambda u(g)
+$$
+
+for drive amplitude $g$. SHIFT-SUV changes the geometry distribution.
+Positive homogeneity in boundary drive cannot guarantee geometry-family
+generalization. The same category error affects the quadratic arm.
+
+The eventual pattern metric makes the mistake visible: homogeneous mode
+changed output amplitude by orders of magnitude while offset-free
+pattern error remained 0.990–0.999. The program was improving a symptom
+on an axis orthogonal to the failed task.
+
+Homogeneous and quadratic modes remain legitimate scoped API contracts
+for problems known to have those drive laws and within the tested
+magnitude/sign domains. They are not transfer repairs.
+
+##### The resolution experiment changed sources and queries together
+
+Trace-mode evaluation uses the sampled surface as both source and query.
+The 2.5k-to-40k sweep therefore changes:
+
+- the empirical source measure;
+- source geometry coverage;
+- query locations;
+- the set over which error is aggregated.
+
+The notebook disclosed this in advance, then later called the
+coverage-specific compensation interpretation unambiguous. The observed
+U-shape and 40k blow-up are strong. The claimed mechanism is not. A
+fixed-source/fixed-query crossed experiment is a cheap discriminator for
+a non-trace path, but it is incompatible with the flagship trace
+contract, where query $i$ must remain source cell $i$. The missed
+design was therefore a non-trace fixed-query compatibility arm plus
+aligned trace evaluations scored on an independently fixed reference
+surface.
+
+##### The exact-estimator comparison mixed different problems
+
+H-CC was motivated by comparing 0.167 trained DrivAer pressure error to
+roughly 0.337 sampling error from an exact-estimator sphere experiment.
+Those are not the same target density, geometry, learned coefficients,
+query distribution, or error decomposition. The arithmetic cannot prove
+that the trained model learned a coverage-specific correction.
+
+The later random-init simple-random-decimation probe is useful evidence,
+but it does not reproduce the production cyclic-block sampler, and one
+small model, sphere, drive, and query cannot establish:
+
+- an 8% production bias;
+- universal $k^{-1/2}$ decay;
+- a 700,000-panel requirement;
+- categorical impossibility of any weighted or control-variate
+  estimator;
+- causality for the trained industrial resolution cliff.
+
+Those are hypotheses for the next experiment, not results of the last
+one.
+
+##### “Preregistered” was treated as stronger than it was
+
+Several preregistration/verdict commits are separated by minutes, and
+the branch is 33 commits ahead of its remote. Local Git author dates are
+useful lab history, not immutable external registration. H-GC/H-MW put
+predictions and results in the same commit. Transfer-v2 changed its
+headline panel after seed 44 failed gate 0. These deviations were
+usually disclosed, which is good, but the correct evidence label is
+“prospectively written exploratory analysis,” not independent
+confirmation.
+
+Future decision rules should be content-addressed and externally
+timestamped before jobs begin.
+
+##### The WSS “seal” is not sealed
+
+The book gives contradictory definitions of `wss_l2`: first a
+whole-field Frobenius relative norm, later a pointwise mean of per-cell
+relative vector errors. Current code and tests pin the Frobenius
+definition. The machine-readable row does not record the evaluation
+SHA, and raw predictions are absent.
+
+The stated $p$-value has no auditable sampling model. Shared integer
+seed labels across different architectures do not automatically create
+matched experimental units; conversely, common data order and other
+randomness can make independence false. The rank-sum result assumes
+independence without preserving the information needed to check it. If
+the runs were prospectively paired by common random numbers, five
+same-sign differences would give a minimum two-sided exact sign-test
+$p=0.0625$. That is one conservative analysis, not proof that pairing
+is the only valid model.
+Absolute WSS errors still favor GeoTransolver by about 2.5–2.7×; the
+reported effect is only a lower *relative degradation ratio*.
+
+The appropriate status is **provisional within-family ratio effect,
+pending metric reconstruction and a declared dependence model**, not sealed
+generalization superiority.
+
